### Problem

Generate all binary strings of length `n` such that no two consecutive characters are `1`.

### Approach

* Use **recursion + backtracking**.
* If the current string ends with `0`, we can add either `0` or `1`.
* If it ends with `1`, we can only add `0`.
* When the string reaches length `n`, add it to the answer.
* After each recursive call, remove the added character using `pop_back()` to backtrack.

### Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> ans;

    vector<string> generateBinaryStrings(int n) {
        string s;
        helper(s, n);
        return ans;
    }

    void helper(string &s, int n) {
        if (s.size() == n) {
            ans.push_back(s);
            return;
        }

        if (s.empty() || s.back() == '0') {
            s.push_back('0');
            helper(s, n);
            s.pop_back();

            s.push_back('1');
            helper(s, n);
            s.pop_back();
        }
        else {
            s.push_back('0');
            helper(s, n);
            s.pop_back();
        }
    }
};
```

### Time Complexity

`O(2^n)` in the worst case, since we generate all valid binary strings.

### Space Complexity

`O(n)` recursion stack, excluding the output.
